# Infrastructure

<!-- Part of the infra stack pack: `pnpm stack:remove infra` removes it. Cloud-neutral: no provider's modules ship. -->

Comment format for `.tf`, `Dockerfile`, and workflows: `docs-infra.mdc`.

## Who runs what

- **Agents write infrastructure; a human applies it.** `terraform`/`tofu` `apply` and `destroy`,
  `state rm` / `state push`, `workspace delete`, `init -migrate-state` / `-force-copy`, and any
  cloud CLI delete (`aws … delete-*`/`terminate-*`/`purge-*`/`deregister-*`, `s3 rm`/`rb`/`mv` from
  a bucket, `gcloud … delete`, `az … delete`/`purge`, `gsutil rm`/`rsync -d`, `bq rm`, disabling
  or destroying a KMS key) are refused by `scripts/command-guard.mjs` in every spelling it can
  read. Editing state (`state mv`, `import`, `taint`, `force-unlock`) and `terraform test` (it
  applies real resources) ask the user. Never route around a refusal (`core.mdc`). What the guard
  can't see (a package script, a Makefile, `docker run hashicorp/terraform`) is still yours not to
  run: `docs/deferred.md` → "Command-guard gaps".
- **Agents run** `fmt`, `validate`, `init -backend=false`, and `plan`. A change's PR carries the
  `plan` summary, every `destroy` / `must be replaced` line named and explained, and the exact
  apply command. The apply is a Human check (`plans.mdc`), run from `docs/runbooks/deploy.md`
  scenario 1 (copy `docs/runbooks/_deploy.md` the first time).
- `plan -destroy` is fine to run; it shows, it doesn't do.

## Terraform layout

- One directory per root module (`infra/`, or `infra/<env>/`), split by concern: `versions.tf`
  (Terraform and provider pins, the provider block), `variables.tf`, `locals.tf`, `outputs.tf`,
  then one file per resource group (`network.tf`, `registry.tf`, `oidc.tf`, `database.tf`).
- Every variable and output has a `description` and a `type`; tflint fails one without.
- `terraform.tfvars.example` is committed with safe placeholders; `terraform.tfvars`, `*.tfstate`,
  `.terraform/`, and plan files never are (`.gitignore`). Commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`.
- Remote state with locking, in a bucket or backend whose deletion protection is on. State is the
  recovery record: losing it orphans every resource it tracked.
- Secrets never go in `.tf` or tfvars. Terraform creates the secret's name and access; the deploy
  workflow writes the value.
- `lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }` on anything holding user data (databases, buckets,
  encryption keys), with a why-comment. Removing it is its own reviewed PR.

## Deploys

- CI deploys with **OIDC**: the job asks the cloud for a short-lived role, never a stored access
  key. The deploy job's permissions are exactly `id-token: write` and `contents: read`. The role
  trusts one repo and one branch or environment, never `repo:<org>/*`.
- `concurrency` with `cancel-in-progress: false` on deploys: a cancelled rollout leaves a
  half-applied service.
- A rollback redeploys an image already in the registry by tag, skipping build and migrations.
  Keep the registry's lifecycle policy long enough to hold the last few good images.
- A shape to copy (AWS; GCP's `google-github-actions/auth` and Azure's `azure/login` take the same
  place):

```yaml
permissions:
  id-token: write # OIDC token for the cloud role
  contents: read
jobs:
  deploy:
    concurrency: { group: deploy-production, cancel-in-progress: false }
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v5
        with:
          role-to-assume: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_ROLE_ARN }} # Terraform output; the role trusts repo:<org>/<repo>:ref:refs/heads/main
          aws-region: ${{ vars.AWS_REGION }}
```

## Docker

- Multi-stage: deps, build, then a slim runner that runs as a non-root user. File header as in
  `docs-infra.mdc` (`# syntax=` stays line 1).
- **Pin the base image** to a version, with a why-comment (why this version, what moving it
  needs). Never `latest` or an untagged image: hadolint fails both (`.hadolint.yaml`).
- `.dockerignore` keeps secrets, state, `.git`, and dependencies out of the build context.
- Build-time secrets via BuildKit `--mount=type=secret`, never `ARG`/`ENV`: a build arg is in
  `docker history` forever.

## Staging

- For what CI can't prove (migrations, the Dockerfile, infrastructure): stood up on demand,
  torn down nightly by a scheduled workflow, so a forgotten stand-up can't run up a bill.
- **A teardown's targets are literals** (`ECS_SERVICE: app-staging`), never `vars.*`, an input,
  or a computed name, and its script refuses any name that isn't staging's. A teardown that read
  repo-level variables once resolved to production and deleted it, nightly.
- Staging has its own state (workspace or directory), its own database, its own OIDC role scoped
  to the staging workflow, and its own secrets prefix. It never reads production's.

## Checks

- **Per edit** (`stacks/infra/check-edited.mjs`): `terraform fmt -check` on a `.tf`/`.tfvars`,
  `hadolint` on any Dockerfile spelling (`Dockerfile.prod`, `api.Dockerfile`, `Containerfile.dev`).
  Offline and fast.
- **`pnpm check` and CI** (`infra:fmt`, `infra:validate`, `infra:lint`, `infra:docker`): fmt,
  `validate` per module after `init -backend=false`, `tflint` per module (`.tflint.hcl`), and
  `hadolint` (`.hadolint.yaml`). No Terraform or Dockerfile yet: each passes and says so.
- Tools are pinned in `stacks/infra/install-tools.sh` (CI runs it). Locally a missing tool is a
  loud SKIPPED line; in CI it fails.
- A cloud's own tflint ruleset (`tflint-ruleset-aws` …) is the project's addition to `.tflint.hcl`, pinned.
